00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.010
The reality is that a lot of those hacks

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.320
are not the most sophisticated hacks

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.500
that you would see in
the cybersecurity space.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.710
A lot of them really rely
on social engineering

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:20.940
and that's kind of the
entry point for the hackers

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.620
to obtain access to the internal systems,

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.100
the IT infrastructure, of
the compromised target.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.220
In social engineering attacks, the victim,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.890
which is usually the
employee of the company,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:37.800
is being persuaded by the hackers

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.580
through some kind of an
email message, a phone call,

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.210
sometimes even a video
chat to do some interaction

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:49.920
which will open malicious files
on their desktop or laptop.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.740
I've been writing forever.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.050
And I've been sending cold emails,

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.380
at least sending one a day
for more than 10 years now.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:01.860
So I've definitely written
more than 10,000 cold emails.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.260
And once that file is open,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.250
the attackers are able to
implant and to install a malware

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.340
that once installed
provides them full access

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:12.510
to that computer.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:17.280
I like to do what I call
e-stalking. Going on LinkedIn.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.230
Twitter works too if they're on Twitter

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:22.230
And if that computer is
not properly isolated

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.570
and has access to the entire network,

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:29.100
they're able to propagate to
other components in the network

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.190
or to access other really
sensitive resources.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.230
And a little pro tip for LinkedIn

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:37.260
is to look on their references.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.260
Who they're referring and
who has referred them.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.810
Because those are actually
really rich with keywords

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.000
and gives you a good
sense of how they talk.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.610
That was also the case
in the Bitfinex hacks

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:48.570
but also like, you know,

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.940
in many hacks that you see even today.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.520
Heather embarks on a multitude

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.120
