shove something up my ass?

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.750
When he decided to propose to her,

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:58.140
he essentially hired a marketing agency

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:03.140
to put up posters of Razzlekhan
around New York City.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.880
The campaign described Heather as someone

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:09.247
who captured the essence of Razzlekhan.

00:14:09.247 --> 00:14:12.703
"Surreal, mysterious, creepy, and sexy?"

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.100
I think the fact that they really sort of

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.910
craved that attention was
also visible at their wedding

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.190
with social media videos that circulated

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.140
from the day of the ceremony

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.930
showing Heather rapping
throughout the ceremony.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.420
But perhaps the most interesting thing was

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:32.610
that she was carried down the aisle

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.160
while sitting on a
throne, by eight people,

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.863
while "Final Countdown" was
playing in the background.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.980
Heather and Dutch, they
would've known something was up

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:45.270
because they'd been notified

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:47.430
by their internet service provider

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:50.250
that someone had asked for their records

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:51.660
through a legal request.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.870
After the hack, Bitfinex
publicized the addresses

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.120
that the hackers had used.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:59.580
This was to put everybody on alert

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:03.930
so that they didn't do any
deals with those bitcoins.

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:06.900
It's almost like setting off the dye pack

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.770
in the bag of stolen money
after a bank robbery.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:13.470
So the hackers had all these Bitcoins,

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:15.930
but it'd be difficult to find someone

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:17.250
who wanted to handle them

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.953
because now they're sort
of marked as dirty money.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.510
So they needed some sort
of less ethical player

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.910
that didn't mind accepting payments

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.160
from their marked accounts.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:33.060
And for five months the
coins didn't move at all.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:36.480
Then they found somebody who was willing

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.310
to take at least some of them.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.910
This less picky business
was called AlphaBay.

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:44.910
Kind of like a eBay on the dark web

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:49.260
where you can buy drugs, guns, whatever.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:53.010
So obviously the administrator
of this underworld eBay

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.350
tried to keep his identity secret.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:58.290
As it turned out, it was
a 25 year old Canadian

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:02.790
named Alexander Cazes who
was living in Thailand.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.640
So the Bitfinex hackers first sent funds

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.390
to AlphaBay in January, 2017.

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.400
What they didn't know is that AlphaBay

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:15.480
was already the target of this
international investigation

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.490
called Operation Bayonet.

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:20.100
And just a few months later in July,

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:25.080
Royal Thai Police raided Cazes's compound

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:29.850
and so they were able to
access all of AlphaBay's files.

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.290
When you send the money to AlphaBay,

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.330
you're like sending it into this box

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.610
and when you withdraw
it no one can tell like

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.020
where it came from exactly.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:40.170
But now the FBI has the box,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:42.780
they look inside and they
can make all the connections

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.271
between the deposits and the withdrawals.

00:16:46.271 --> 00:16:48.087
So if you are able to track

00:16:48.087 --> 00:16:50.040
the cryptocurrency transactions

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.560
and eventually the hacker is sloppy,

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:56.490
or didn't really think about
how they're going to cash out,

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:59.250
the cryptocurrency that
they were able to steal

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:02.040
then they would authenticate themselves

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.980
with a financial institution,

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:06.930
let's say a centralized exchange,

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.970
a FinTech app or something like that,

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:11.100
through which they will try to convert

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:14.430
say, the bitcoins, to regular cash.

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:18.030
The police or the law enforcement
agencies can now identify

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:21.510
who was trying to basically
cash out the funds.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:25.350
At least in some cases, some
of the money went to accounts

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:29.430
that had been opened by
Dutch using his real name.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.140
Presumably he thought this would never

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:35.340
be traced back to the hacked funds.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.960
But the raid on AlphaBay
I think made it possible

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:41.776
to make the connection.

00:17:41.776 --> 00:17:45.960
94,000 Bitcoin, that is $3.6 billion.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:47.490
On Tuesday, police in New York

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.200
arrested a husband and wife couple

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.080
that allegedly tried to
launder just that amount.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.730
When the indictment came out-

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.260
I got a text message

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:58.350
from my business partner being
like, you need to see this.

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:01.140
I clicked on it and I saw a picture.

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:05.430
It just popped up right there
saying, okay, such and such.

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:08.940
But then I said, Heather
Morgan, I know that name.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:10.440
And then when I clicked on it, you know,

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:12.940
their photo was there. I
was like, oh my goodness.

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:15.180
This blew my mind.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.360
I immediately messaged her
like, "Dude, what's going on?"

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.420
It was January 5th, 2022.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.310
Heather's parents were visiting

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:26.760
and obviously Heather
and Dutch had no idea

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:29.430
that these agents were coming on this day.

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:31.830
These FBI and IRS agents

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:34.320
come into Heather and Dutch's apartment,

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.960
start taking their electronic devices.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.740
The agents described the
search in a court filing

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:42.757
and at some point Heather says,

00:18:42.757 --> 00:18:45.600
"This is taking a long
time. We'd like to leave,

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:49.650
but can we take our Bengal
cat Clarissa with us?"

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:52.590
She crouches down next to the bed

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:55.050
and tries to get Clarissa out.

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:57.450
But it was actually kind of a trick

00:18:57.450 --> 00:19:00.210
'cause what she really wanted
to do was grab her cell phone

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:03.330
off the nightstand, she
starts hitting lock,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:04.890
but the agents are right there,

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:06.690
able to get the cell phone from her.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:09.480
They found burner phones,
hollowed-out books,

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.760
all sorts of electronics.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.950
The authorities had also gotten warrants

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:18.000
to search Dutch's cloud storage accounts.

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.550
And in this, they found evidence

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:24.150
that they'd been
preparing fake identities,

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.940
it looked like maybe they were
planning to flee the country.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:29.610
And by the end of the
month, on January 31st,

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:34.260
they were able to crack the
encryption to one of his files

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:39.260
which had all the passwords
to the Bitcoin wallets

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.373
that held the stolen Bitcoins.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:43.800
This was like the jackpot.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.142
This is what they were looking for

00:19:46.142 --> 00:19:48.420
because it doesn't necessarily prove

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.970
that Dutch was the hacker

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:55.590
but somehow he'd come into
possession of these passwords

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:58.470
and whoever has the passwords,
controls the Bitcoins.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.500
And by this point, they're
worth billions of dollars.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:02.670
And so all of a sudden,

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:07.670
these two quirky, awkward
individuals that post seemingly,

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:11.100
you know, innocent videos
across social media,

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:14.160
have become these two individuals

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:19.160
that allegedly conspire to
launder $4.5 billion in crypto,

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:21.600
what the DOJ describes

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.633
as their largest financial seizure ever.

00:20:35.379 --> 00:20:37.384
I couldn't believe it.

00:20:37.384 --> 00:20:42.384
It didn't seem real,
knowing the kind of person

00:20:42.438 --> 00:20:44.670
she is.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.460
She's kind, she's thoughtful,
she's interesting,

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.282
she's all these things but maybe she

00:20:50.282 --> 00:20:53.040
doesn't realize that this isn't a movie.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.890
I'm not defending anything. Right?

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.250
People do weird things
and some take it too far.

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:00.780
Yo, razzle dazzle.

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:02.310
In terms of general scrutiny

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:03.960
that the women have to go through

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:08.220
is definitely much harder than guys.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.380
And that's the same with Heather's case,

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.630
now I see is that everybody's just focused

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:14.370
on the entertainment aspect of it.

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:16.950
Do you ever wanna get f--ked up?

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:20.400
First thing that came out
is all about, 'oh, you know,

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:23.100
look at, you know, the
rapping and this and that.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:24.390
Probably crazy.'

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:27.030
So immediately the objectivity

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:29.163
of the case itself is kind of lost.

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:35.400
In the crypto world almost
nothing shocks me anymore

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.850
but this was definitely pretty surprising.

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.250
This is a world where all sorts of peoples

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:44.223
are getting rich and
then losing it overnight.

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.250
You have a crypto scam,

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.700
you have TikTok videos going viral.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:53.460
You have that hype and that need

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.510
to be sort of a celebrity
across social media.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:21:59.490
And so really these are all the forces

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:02.760
that came together to
create a crypto heist

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:04.863
that was made for the digital times.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:08.310
I would say that the big exchanges

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:10.650
seem to have tightened their security

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:14.340
but then the hackers have
also stepped up their game.

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:16.410
We are seeing the hackers really moving

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:21.090
into the area of DeFi
protocol, Web3 organizations,

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:24.090
gaming companies that are
using cryptocurrencies

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:26.100
because they're the new targets for them.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:29.130
The fact that social
engineering still happens today

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:32.790
and it's the number one entry point.

00:22:32.790 --> 00:22:34.950
That's definitely true.

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:37.530
I went down to the
courthouse where Heather

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:40.980
and Dutch would've gone
for their pretrial hearing.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.200
And on a traffic pole right across

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:45.630
from where she likely
would've walked into court,

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:48.570
there was a sticker, one of the same ones

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:50.760
that she'd posted all over
the financial district,

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:54.540
that showed Razzlekhan riding a crocodile.

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:56.940
Who knows, maybe she's
riding the crocodile

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:58.384
off into the sunset.

00:22:58.384 --> 00:23:00.352
♪ We're too weird for average Joes ♪

00:23:00.352 --> 00:23:03.316
♪ Everyone knows you're the best for me ♪

00:23:03.316 --> 00:23:05.475
♪ This is how our story goes ♪

00:23:05.475 --> 00:23:08.747
♪ This is the Razzlekhan
and Dutchie shows ♪

00:23:08.747 --> 00:23:12.825
♪ Ready to party down
and let's get weird ♪

00:23:12.825 --> 00:23:15.429
♪ Moon and stars, moon and stars, moon ♪

00:23:15.429 --> 00:23:17.385
